ave no indexes. If they do, some of this changes.\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`, from `lower(u.email)`.** Wrapping the column in a function means a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database has to compute `lower()` for every row. This is the main cost.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, or a hash join over it, on `o.user_id`.** Without an index on `orders.user_id`, the database can't look up one user's orders directly. It reads all of `orders` or builds a hash table from it.\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** Every column of both tables is returned, so the query can never be answered from an index alone. It also repeats the user's columns on every order row, and the duplicate `id` and `created_at` columns can collide in client code.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n1. Index the expression so the lookup becomes an index seek (PostgreSQL syntax shown):\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   Alternatively, store emails already lowercased, or use a case-insensitive type or collation such as `citext`. Then the query can use `WHERE u.email = ...` with an ordinary unique index. This is cleaner if you can enforce it on write.\n2. Index the join key:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n   ```\n3. List only the columns you need:\n   ```sql\n   SELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total   -- example columns\n   FROM orders o\n   JOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\n   WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n   ```\n   If the caller needs only orders, select only `o.*`-style columns. If it also needs the user, fetch the user once and don't repeat it on every order row.\n\nWith both indexes, the plan should be one seek on `users` and then one range seek on `orders`, instead of two full scans. Check with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)` before and after, because the benefit depends on table size. Tiny tables won't show a difference.\n\nIf you share the schema and your database engine, I can tailor the index syntax and pick the exact column list.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}